evidence about the safety and  effectiveness of covet-19 vaccination  during pregnancy has been growing and  here to tell us more is women's health  service physician dr nica vascara from  intermountain health care the sponsors  of the cell segment welcome dr vascara  thank you for being here so many  questions about pregnancy and  vaccinations can you what can you tell  me are you seeing a difference in covent  infection rates between vaccinated and  unvaccinated women  yes well firstly thank you so much for  having me here i'm very excited to be  here to talk about this important topic  today  regarding your question there absolutely  is a difference  especially when we're talking about code  infection with um that leads to in-house  our hospitalization and icu admission we  actually have seen through the cdc cases  reported that symptomatic patients with  coven-19 infections who are pregnant  have a three-fold increase in  risk of  intensive care unit admission or icu  admission which includes ventilation  support as well as a 70 increased risk  of  death when compared to non-pregnant  patients now i know a lot of women are  very concerned especially in that first  trimester  of just doing everything right because  you know that's a very cautious time for  a lot of women is it better to perhaps  wait until the second trimester or to  get a vaccination if that's something  you're interested in or  should you just do it as soon as you can  that's a really excellent question so  basically through all of the evidence  regarding the safety and effectiveness  of vaccines of covered vaccine  specifically in pregnancy  we found that the  the  the known benefits of vaccination in  pregnancy far outweigh the potential  risks of vaccination and pregnancy so we  recommend it  and we recommend the primary treatment  or primary vaccination in all stages of  pregnancy let's talk about boosters the  big topic of conversation right now a  lot of people are looking to get their  b